# Sonar MCP Server — Architecture & Rebuild Guide

This document describes how the Sonar MCP Server is built and gives everything needed to recreate it from an empty directory. Part A explains the architecture. Part B is a step-by-step rebuild with the full contents of every file. Part C lists known gaps in the current code that you may want to fix while rebuilding.

---

# Part A — Architecture

## A.1 What the system does

The project is a **Model Context Protocol (MCP) server** written in Java 21 / Spring Boot 3.4 / Spring AI 1.0. An AI coding agent (GitHub Copilot in VS Code, or JetBrains AI Assistant) launches it as a child process and talks to it over **stdin/stdout using JSON-RPC** (the MCP STDIO transport).

The server exposes two tools:

| Tool | Purpose | Side effects |
|---|---|---|
| `sonar_list_issues` | Read-only query of SonarQube issues, enriched with rule descriptions | None |
| `sonar_fix_issues` | Same data as `sonar_list_issues`, but its **tool description is an 11-step prompt** that tells the agent to fix the issues, build, commit, and open an Azure DevOps PR | None on the server; the *agent* performs all edits, git and PR actions |

The key design idea: **the server only fetches data. The fixing workflow lives in the tool description**, which the agent reads as instructions. The server never touches the user's code, git, or Azure DevOps.

