## B.13 Configuration reference

| Env var | Property | Required | Default | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `SONAR_URL` | `sonar.url` | yes | — | Base URL, no trailing `/api` |
| `SONAR_TOKEN` | `sonar.token` | yes | — | Sent as `Bearer` |
| `SONAR_DEFAULT_PROJECT_KEY` | `sonar.default-project-key` | no | — | `CLOUD_SERVICE:` prefix added automatically |
| `SONAR_SSL_VERIFY` | `sonar.ssl-verify` | no | `true` | `false` disables cert + hostname checks |

Tool parameter defaults: severities `BLOCKER,CRITICAL,MAJOR`; types `VULNERABILITY,BUG`; statuses `OPEN,CONFIRMED,REOPENED`; page `1`; page size `10` (max 500).

## B.14 Extending the server

To add a tool:
1. Add a method to `SonarQubeClient` for the new SonarQube endpoint (reuse `get`).
2. Add a public method to `SonarQubeService` annotated `@Tool(name = "sonar_...", description = "...")` with `@ToolParam` parameters. Return a JSON `String`; catch exceptions and return `errorResponse(...)`.
3. Mention the tool in `spring.ai.mcp.server.instructions` and in the startup log line in `SonarMcpServerApplication`.

No registration code is needed; `MethodToolCallbackProvider` discovers all `@Tool` methods on the service.

